1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:02,100
 Now that we saw the main components of an Android

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:05,860
 application, there is one last, but very important aspect of

3
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:08,610
 Android that we need to cover, which is the Android

4
00:00:08,610 --> 00:00:12,470
 activity lifecycle. So I'm going to use this classic diagram

5
00:00:12,470 --> 00:00:16,621
 to illustrate such lifecycle. But before getting to describing the

6
00:00:16,621 --> 00:00:18,875
 different parts, let's see why we have a lifecycle in

7
00:00:18,875 --> 00:00:21,730
 the first place. The point here is that because Android

8
00:00:21,730 --> 00:00:25,510
 apps run in a multitasking environment, the runtime system routinely

9
00:00:25,510 --> 00:00:30,160
 suspends, stops, and even kills applications and activities. If

10
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:31,920
 you want an example, just think of the case of

11
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:33,850
 a phone call that arrives while you're writing an

12
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:36,630
 email message. In such cases, what happens is that the

13
00:00:36,630 --> 00:00:40,430
 phone application replaces your email client and gets in

14
00:00:40,430 --> 00:00:43,632
 the forefront. So apps must be able to react to

15
00:00:43,632 --> 00:00:47,520
 these occurrences and suitably handle them. Luckily, the Android system

16
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:50,590
 is nice enough to notify activities when it needs to

17
00:00:50,590 --> 00:00:53,712
 change their state, for example, when it needs to suspend, or

18
00:00:53,712 --> 00:00:56,950
 kill them. And it does so by calling some special methods in

19
00:00:56,950 --> 00:01:00,800
 the activities. Therefore, when you develop an application, an Android app,

20
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:04,019
 you must be aware of what these methods are, and suitably implement

21
00:01:04,019 --> 00:01:06,620
 them. We will therefore now have a look at these methods,

22
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:09,710
 and see what they mean when they get called, and how this

23
00:01:09,710 --> 00:01:12,792
 relates to the different states of an activity. And we're going to

24
00:01:12,792 --> 00:01:15,720
 do that using this diagram that I introduced here. In order to

25
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:18,140
 better illustrate how this all works I'm going to

26
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:21,920
 complicate the picture a little bit by adding information about

27
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:26,080
 what happens when going from one phase of the lifecycle

28
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:28,150
 to another one. Because this will have to make the

29
00:01:28,150 --> 00:01:31,630
 diagram more self-explanatory. Let's start from here, from the

30
00:01:31,630 --> 00:01:34,340
 moment when an activity, or an app is launched. We

31
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:37,140
 will call our activity A, and what happens is that

32
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:40,900
 when A is started, the system will call different methods

33
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:44,340
 on the activity, and will put the activity in the foreground.

34
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:46,490
 What that means is that the activity will stay on the

35
00:01:46,490 --> 00:01:49,600
 screen, visible, the user will be able to interact with the

36
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:53,660
 activity, and the activity will be in the running state. And so

37
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:56,140
 this is when you're looking at your device and you are

38
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:59,380
 seeing in the foreground your activity and you can interact with it.

39
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:02,590
 At this point two things might happen if another activity is

40
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:06,000
 launched. In the first case A might lose is focus but still

41
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,222
 be visible. This is the case in which the new

42
00:02:08,222 --> 00:02:11,280
 activity that is being created is maybe an activity that doesn't

43
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:13,750
 take up the whole screen, so my original activity is still

44
00:02:13,750 --> 00:02:16,875
 visible in the background. Or a case in which the activity

45
00:02:16,875 --> 00:02:19,584
 is a transparent activity, so it has focus but I can

46
00:02:19,584 --> 00:02:22,920
 still see the original activity behind it. In this case the

47
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:26,960
 system will call onPause on Activity A. And the activity will

48
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:31,280
 be in the paused state. A paused activity is completely alive

49
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,900
 in the sense that it maintains all state information and

50
00:02:33,900 --> 00:02:36,850
 remains attached to the window manager. But what can happen is

51
00:02:36,850 --> 00:02:40,710
 that the activity might be killed or destroyed for example in

52
00:02:40,710 --> 00:02:43,620
 case in which there is not enough memory to run both

53
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:47,030
 the new activity and the original activity. So the second case

54
00:02:47,030 --> 00:02:49,550
 is a case in which the new activity that is being

55
00:02:49,550 --> 00:02:53,550
 launched is completely obscuring my original activity. Which is a kind

56
00:02:53,550 --> 00:02:56,280
 of more standard case. So, for example, you receive a phone

57
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:59,380
 call while you're editing a message. The phone app completely

58
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:03,300
 replaces the email client on your device, and becomes the

59
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:06,280
 new running activity, the one in the foreground. In this

60
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:09,470
 case the system will call onStop on the original activity and

61
00:03:09,470 --> 00:03:11,930
 the activity will be in the stop state. Similar to

62
00:03:11,930 --> 00:03:15,480
 the pause state, also in this case the activity A

63
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:18,110
 still retains all of state information but it is more

64
00:03:18,110 --> 00:03:21,290
 likely that it will be killed or destroyed by the system.

65
00:03:21,290 --> 00:03:23,640
 So let's look at what can happen from these two states

66
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:26,520
 in a little more detail. As we said there are cases

67
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:30,490
 in which an app with higher priority might need memory from

68
00:03:30,490 --> 00:03:33,655
 the system, resources from the system. And the activity that's paused or

69
00:03:33,655 --> 00:03:36,800
 stopped will be killed. In this case, if the user happens

70
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:39,580
 to go back to the activity, the system will recreate it

71
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:42,710
 basically, so it will restart it from scratch. So this is

72
00:03:42,710 --> 00:03:46,310
 the reason why it is very important that when your application goes

73
00:03:46,310 --> 00:03:48,900
 into the paused or stopped state, which means when the

74
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:52,970
 system calls onPause or onStop in your app, you suitably save

75
00:03:52,970 --> 00:03:55,010
 the state of the application so that the state can

76
00:03:55,010 --> 00:03:58,980
 be restored if the activity is killed and recreated afterwards. A

77
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:02,180
 second possibility when your activity is paused or stopped is

78
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:05,430
 that the activity is finishing or is being completely destroyed by

79
00:04:05,430 --> 00:04:08,410
 the system. In this case, the onDestroy method is called and

80
00:04:08,410 --> 00:04:11,390
 the activity is shut down for good. The third possibility is

81
00:04:11,390 --> 00:04:14,620
 that the activity is not killed, is not destroyed, and the user

82
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:17,850
 simply returns to the activity. For example, you go back to editing

83
00:04:17,850 --> 00:04:21,180
 your email after finishing your phone call. In this case, the system

84
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:24,880
 will call onRestart() on the activity and the activity will go back

85
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:27,390
 to the running state. So what I just covered are kind of

86
00:04:27,390 --> 00:04:31,410
 the main paths through an activity lifecycle. But I'll strongly encourage you

87
00:04:31,410 --> 00:04:33,490
 to read more about this, and to make sure to go through

88
00:04:33,490 --> 00:04:36,690
 this lifecycle until it is clear to you what the different states

89
00:04:36,690 --> 00:04:38,710
 are, and what you should do for each of them

90
00:04:38,710 --> 00:04:38,710
 when the system invokes state changing methods on your activity.

